WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:09.090
By the 1950s the lock-and-key picture decorated every textbook and Daniel Koshland

2
00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.110
knew it was incomplete, because of water.

3
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:16.993
If an active site sat permanently open and waiting, water molecules, smaller than

4
00:00:16.993 --> 00:00:20.793
any substrate, should slip in constantly and trigger chaos.

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:22.433
They don't.

6
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:27.527
So something must change when the real substrate arrives: the hand doesn't just

7
00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:31.987
slide into a pre-formed glove; the glove changes shape around the hand.

8
00:00:32.927 --> 00:00:38.087
Binding induces a conformational change "induced fit" and water is too small to

9
00:00:38.087 --> 00:00:39.087
force it.

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.400
The idea cut so hard against the dogma that standard journals blocked it (one

11
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.420
reviewer's verdict became famous); Koshland slipped it into PNAS in 1958 through a

12
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:51.420
loophole.

13
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:57.543
X-ray crystallography later proved him right: enzymes flex, shift, and clamp down.

14
00:00:58.003 --> 00:01:01.903
He had moved biochemistry from a science of static structures to one of moving

15
00:01:01.903 --> 00:01:02.323
machines.
